{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RQ4x5Y8cmt7xyL2QLdj71p","type":"tool_result","content":"If $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\ngraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\nindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n\\end{lemma}\nThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in\nI$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$\npatterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion\nand the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns\nfollows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant\n$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\ncite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$\nand the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at\n30--100 customers. The sandwich\n$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\nwidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest. The best published upper bound we know,\nthe degree-sequence bound of \\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, is\nwider still on the cover degree law, $0.66$, $0.86$ and $0.96$ at $d=4$, $6$,\n$10$ with $\\mu=1$, because it sees the degrees of a union of cliques and not\nthe cliques. The greedy closing rule we use for\nupper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per\ncustomer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve).\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\nfirst-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the\nslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100\ncustomers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of\nthe analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up\nto concentration).}\n\\label{tab:excess}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n\\end{table}\n\n======\nThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\nsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\nprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\nstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\nused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\nChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\nthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\nThree more things are known. The best published upper bound on the pathwidth\nof $G(n,c/n)$ is the degree-sequence bound of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015},\nwhich on Poisson degrees gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$ at $c=3,6,10$,\nso the measured slopes sit near the middle of the sandwich between\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ and it. The prefix-at-$(1-\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the\nempty-pair argument is published by \\citet{CheungGirardet2020}, who reach\npathwidth $(1-o(1))n$ for every $p\\ge n^{-1+\\varepsilon}$, still nothing at\n$p=c/n$. And a sharp constant for vertex separation is known for one random\nmodel only, dense random geometric graphs \\citep{DiazPenrosePetitSerna2001},\nand for none of $G(n,c/n)$, random regular graphs or random clique covers.\n\nOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\nexactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\nfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\nprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\nis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$. Counting the event a\nnarrow layout forces instead of its witnesses, once per separator or once per\nclosed pair (the paragraph \\emph{Why the first moment is loose} in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:excess}), closes a third of it at $c=3$ and a tenth at $c=6$\nand $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on\n$\\pw/n$ is $[0.037,0.183]$, $[0.182,0.458]$ and $[0.345,0.656]$ there, against\nfitted slopes of $0.099$, $0.268$ and $0.415$.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97b23e8b-c280-4820-b777-f4948c9a83ab","uuid":"eb9f862f-70c3-4211-9d47-d377f4b72a38","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:16:26.630Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"If $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\ngraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\nindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n\\end{lemma}\nThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in\nI$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$\npatterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion\nand the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns\nfollows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant\n$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\ncite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$\nand the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at\n30--100 customers. The sandwich\n$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\nwidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest. The best published upper bound we know,\nthe degree-sequence bound of \\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, is\nwider still on the cover degree law, $0.66$, $0.86$ and $0.96$ at $d=4$, $6$,\n$10$ with $\\mu=1$, because it sees the degrees of a union of cliques and not\nthe cliques. The greedy closing rule we use for\nupper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per\ncustomer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve).\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\nfirst-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the\nslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100\ncustomers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of\nthe analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up\nto concentration).}\n\\label{tab:excess}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n\\end{table}\n\n======\nThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\nsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\nprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\nstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\nused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\nChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\nthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\nThree more things are known. The best published upper bound on the pathwidth\nof $G(n,c/n)$ is the degree-sequence bound of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015},\nwhich on Poisson degrees gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$ at $c=3,6,10$,\nso the measured slopes sit near the middle of the sandwich between\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ and it. The prefix-at-$(1-\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the\nempty-pair argument is published by \\citet{CheungGirardet2020}, who reach\npathwidth $(1-o(1))n$ for every $p\\ge n^{-1+\\varepsilon}$, still nothing at\n$p=c/n$. And a sharp constant for vertex separation is known for one random\nmodel only, dense random geometric graphs \\citep{DiazPenrosePetitSerna2001},\nand for none of $G(n,c/n)$, random regular graphs or random clique covers.\n\nOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\nexactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\nfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\nprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\nis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$. Counting the event a\nnarrow layout forces instead of its witnesses, once per separator or once per\nclosed pair (the paragraph \\emph{Why the first moment is loose} in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:excess}), closes a third of it at $c=3$ and a tenth at $c=6$\nand $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on\n$\\pw/n$ is $[0.037,0.183]$, $[0.182,0.458]$ and $[0.345,0.656]$ there, against\nfitted slopes of $0.099$, $0.268$ and $0.415$.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RQ4x5Y8cmt7xyL2QLdj71p","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
