{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hcwi8cUParq5vZNWNNqpHJ","type":"tool_result","content":"---\n\n## 6. Not verified, or not read in full\n\n- Kneis et al. 2009: abstract only (Crossref); `13/75` confirmed only through\n  Edwards–McDermid's restatement.\n- Esparza–Luttenberger–Schlund's `pw − 1 ≤ S ≤ 2 pw`: stated in the paper\n  without proof or leaf convention; checked here on four small families only.\n  The random-tree window of §3.5 rests on it.\n- The re-rooting step `|H_r − H_{r'}| ≤ 1` and the random-cubic upper bound\n  `0.1399 n` via Fomin–Høie's construction: [derived], unrefereed.\n- Fomin–Høie's \"`0.082n` for pathwidth\" from Ramanujan bisection: the transfer\n  is asserted, not written; the consequence for random cubic graphs via\n  Lichev–Mitsche is UNVERIFIED.\n- Kostochka–Melnikov's random-cubic bisection constant: `0.10101 n`\n  (Lichev–Mitsche) against `n/4.95 ≈ 0.202 n` (Bezrukov et al.); not resolved.\n- Edwards–McDermid Corollary 5.2's displayed equality `Σ_{r(G)} = Σ_{d ≥ 3} g n_d + o(n)`:\n  read as \"at most\", justified in §3.3 by monotonicity of `g`; the paper's\n  own `n_d` is not defined on that line.\n- The Poisson evaluation of E–M and FGSS assumes the degree histogram of\n  `G(n, c/n)` converges in `L¹` to Poisson(`c`), standard; the cover-cell\n  evaluation ignores pattern overlaps and is an upper bound on the bound.\n- Cheung–Girardet: arXiv v1 read; the 2020 journal version may differ.\n- Tavakoli 2012: OCR text; Theorem 3.10's statement not legible.\n- Golovnev–Kutzkov, Monien–Preis, Kostochka–Melnikov: as restated by others.\n- Phase transition of tractability (arXiv 1212.2485): search snippet only.\n====SURVEY 1.4====\n**Upper bounds** (any graph, applied to `G(n,c/n)`):\n- **Cyclomatic number** [derived]: `pw ≤ pw(spanning forest) + (m − n + #components)`.\n  The forest term is `O(log n)`. Whp `m − n + κ = n[c/2 − 1 + (x − x²/2)/c] + o(n)`,\n  where `x < 1` solves `x e^{−x} = c e^{−c}` (standard component count). This is\n  best for `1 < c ≲ 2.2` and tends to 0 as `c ↓ 1`, consistent with Do–Erde–Kang's\n  `Θ(ε³n)`.\n- **Kneis, Mölle, Richter & Rossmanith (2009)**, \"A bound on the pathwidth of\n  sparse graphs with applications to exact algorithms\", *SIAM J. Discrete Math.*\n  23(1):407–427, DOI 10.1137/080715482: **`pw ≤ m/5.769 + O(log n)`** for every\n  graph [pub, abstract]. On `G(n,c/n)` this is `pw/n ≤ c/11.54 + o(1)`, best for\n  `2.2 ≲ c ≲ 8`. Edwards & McDermid (2015, *Algorithmica* 72(4):940–968,\n  DOI 10.1007/s00453-014-9883-7) give a degree-sequence bound that matches\n  Fomin et al. up to degree 6 and is smaller beyond. Its closed form was not\n  read (UNVERIFIED).\n- **Fomin, Gaspers, Saurabh & Stepanov**, \"On two techniques of combining\n  branching and treewidth\", *Algorithmica* 54:181–207 (2009),\n  DOI 10.1007/s00453-007-9133-3: `pw ≤ n₃/6 + n₄/3 + 13n₅/30 + 23n₆/45 + n_{≥7} + εn` (coefficients corrected 2026-11-01 from the PDF)\n  [pub, as quoted in search results; coefficient list not read in the PDF].\n- **Independence number** [derived, standard; **proved in Lean 2026-10-30**,\n  `lean/MOSPFormalization/IndependenceBound.lean`,\n  `pathwidth_le_card_sub_indepNum`, against Mathlib's `indepNum`, standard\n  axioms only; brute-force checked on every graph to 9 vertices the same day]:\n  bags `(V∖I) ∪ {v}`, `v ∈ I`, give `pw ≤ n − α(G)`. Random greedy gives `α ≥ (ln(1+c)/c)·n` whp, so\n  `pw/n ≤ 1 − ln(1+c)/c`. (The greedy constant is a classical result; its\n  reference was not re-checked.) As `c → ∞`, `α ≈ 2n ln c / c` (Frieze 1990),\n  so `1 − pw/n ≥ (2 − o(1)) ln c / c`.\n\nSo for large `c`: **`1 − pw/n ∈ [(2 − o(1)) ln c/c, (4 + o(1)) ln c/c]`**\n[derived], the order of `n − pw` is `Θ(n log c / c)`, and the constant in front\n(between 2 and 4) is open.\n\n---\n\n====7a====\n### (a) Bounds on `pw/n` (and `tw/n`) for `G(n, c/n)`, by `c`\n\nLB is the best lower bound, UB the best upper bound, both whp as `n → ∞`, with\n`o(1)` terms dropped. \"PS\" is Perarnau–Serra's tw bound `1 − √(9 ln 3/(2c))`, a\nlower bound on pw too. \"FM\" is our pw first moment `β*(c)` (§1.4). UB is the\nminimum of cyclomatic, Kneis `c/11.54` and `1 − ln(1+c)/c`. The empirical column\nis Gogate–Dechter's exact **tw**/n (a lower estimate of pw/n) at finite `n`.\n\n| `c` | status | LB on pw/n | source | UB on pw/n | source | G–D exact tw/n |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| < 1 | sublinear | `pw = (1+o(1)) log₃ log n` | derived; `O(log log n)` pub (td) | — | — | — |\n| 1 | critical | `pw → ∞`, `≤ (2/3) log₃ n` | derived | — | — | — |\n| `1+ε`, `ε → 0`, `ε³n → ∞` | `Θ(ε³n)` | `0.02 ε³` | DEK §5 (tw) | `≈ 0.67 ε³` (pw), `1.34 ε³` (tw, DEK) | derived / DEK | — |\n| 1.1 | linear, constant unknown | `r(c) > 0`, implicit | LLO, PS, DEK | 0.0006 | cyclomatic | — |\n| 1.5 | \" | implicit | \" | 0.037 | cyclomatic | — |\n| 2 | \" | implicit | \" | 0.085 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.072 (n=50) |\n| 2.5 | \" | implicit (Gao: linear for c > 2.146) | Gao 2012 | 0.132 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 3 | \" | 0.009 | FM | 0.183 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 4 | \" | 0.061 | FM | 0.285 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.216 (n=50), 0.252 (n=25) |\n| 5 | \" | 0.118 | FM (PS: 0.006) | 0.378 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 6 | \" | 0.171 | FM (PS: 0.092) | 0.458 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.346 (n=50) |\n| 8 | \" | 0.263 | FM (PS: 0.214) | 0.576 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.407 (n=50), 0.472 (n=25) |\n| 10 | \" | 0.337 | FM (PS: 0.297) | 0.656 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 12 | \" | 0.397 | FM (PS: 0.358) | 0.711 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.552 (n=50), 0.604 (n=25) |\n| 16 | \" | 0.488 | FM (PS: 0.444) | 0.782 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.674 (n=50), 0.720 (n=25) |\n| 20 | \" | 0.554 | FM (PS: 0.503) | 0.826 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 50 | \" | 0.761 | FM (PS: 0.686) | 0.921 | \" | — |\n| 100 | \" | 0.857 | FM (PS: 0.778) | 0.954 | \" | — |\n| `c → ∞` | | `1 − (4+o(1)) ln c/c` | FM | `1 − (2−o(1)) ln c/c` | `α` (Frieze) | — |\n| `p` const. | | `n − (4+o(1)) log_b n` | derived (pub: `n − O(√(n/p))`, PS) | `n − (2−o(1)) log_b n` | `n − α` | — |\n\nNo sharp constant is known at any `c`. Between `c = 1` and `c ≈ 2.8` there is\n**no explicit numerical lower bound at all**, only existence of `r(c) > 0`. The\nupper/lower gap is a factor of about 2–20 throughout the interesting range `2 ≤ c ≤ 10`\n(the UB column is Edwards–McDermid's degree-sequence bound evaluated on Poisson(c)\ndegrees, `reports/random_graph_pathwidth_literature_2.md`, 2026-11-01; it replaced the\nedge-count and cyclomatic values at `2 ≤ c ≤ 20`).\nGogate–Dechter's finite-`n` treewidths sit inside every interval, and they fall\nwith `n` (`c = 8`: 0.472 at `n = 25`, 0.407 at `n = 50`), which shows how large\nthe finite-size effects are at these sizes.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97b23e8b-c280-4820-b777-f4948c9a83ab","uuid":"0f226695-399b-49d6-8712-57eddebc5d5b","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:16:30.880Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\n\n## 6. Not verified, or not read in full\n\n- Kneis et al. 2009: abstract only (Crossref); `13/75` confirmed only through\n  Edwards–McDermid's restatement.\n- Esparza–Luttenberger–Schlund's `pw − 1 ≤ S ≤ 2 pw`: stated in the paper\n  without proof or leaf convention; checked here on four small families only.\n  The random-tree window of §3.5 rests on it.\n- The re-rooting step `|H_r − H_{r'}| ≤ 1` and the random-cubic upper bound\n  `0.1399 n` via Fomin–Høie's construction: [derived], unrefereed.\n- Fomin–Høie's \"`0.082n` for pathwidth\" from Ramanujan bisection: the transfer\n  is asserted, not written; the consequence for random cubic graphs via\n  Lichev–Mitsche is UNVERIFIED.\n- Kostochka–Melnikov's random-cubic bisection constant: `0.10101 n`\n  (Lichev–Mitsche) against `n/4.95 ≈ 0.202 n` (Bezrukov et al.); not resolved.\n- Edwards–McDermid Corollary 5.2's displayed equality `Σ_{r(G)} = Σ_{d ≥ 3} g n_d + o(n)`:\n  read as \"at most\", justified in §3.3 by monotonicity of `g`; the paper's\n  own `n_d` is not defined on that line.\n- The Poisson evaluation of E–M and FGSS assumes the degree histogram of\n  `G(n, c/n)` converges in `L¹` to Poisson(`c`), standard; the cover-cell\n  evaluation ignores pattern overlaps and is an upper bound on the bound.\n- Cheung–Girardet: arXiv v1 read; the 2020 journal version may differ.\n- Tavakoli 2012: OCR text; Theorem 3.10's statement not legible.\n- Golovnev–Kutzkov, Monien–Preis, Kostochka–Melnikov: as restated by others.\n- Phase transition of tractability (arXiv 1212.2485): search snippet only.\n====SURVEY 1.4====\n**Upper bounds** (any graph, applied to `G(n,c/n)`):\n- **Cyclomatic number** [derived]: `pw ≤ pw(spanning forest) + (m − n + #components)`.\n  The forest term is `O(log n)`. Whp `m − n + κ = n[c/2 − 1 + (x − x²/2)/c] + o(n)`,\n  where `x < 1` solves `x e^{−x} = c e^{−c}` (standard component count). This is\n  best for `1 < c ≲ 2.2` and tends to 0 as `c ↓ 1`, consistent with Do–Erde–Kang's\n  `Θ(ε³n)`.\n- **Kneis, Mölle, Richter & Rossmanith (2009)**, \"A bound on the pathwidth of\n  sparse graphs with applications to exact algorithms\", *SIAM J. Discrete Math.*\n  23(1):407–427, DOI 10.1137/080715482: **`pw ≤ m/5.769 + O(log n)`** for every\n  graph [pub, abstract]. On `G(n,c/n)` this is `pw/n ≤ c/11.54 + o(1)`, best for\n  `2.2 ≲ c ≲ 8`. Edwards & McDermid (2015, *Algorithmica* 72(4):940–968,\n  DOI 10.1007/s00453-014-9883-7) give a degree-sequence bound that matches\n  Fomin et al. up to degree 6 and is smaller beyond. Its closed form was not\n  read (UNVERIFIED).\n- **Fomin, Gaspers, Saurabh & Stepanov**, \"On two techniques of combining\n  branching and treewidth\", *Algorithmica* 54:181–207 (2009),\n  DOI 10.1007/s00453-007-9133-3: `pw ≤ n₃/6 + n₄/3 + 13n₅/30 + 23n₆/45 + n_{≥7} + εn` (coefficients corrected 2026-11-01 from the PDF)\n  [pub, as quoted in search results; coefficient list not read in the PDF].\n- **Independence number** [derived, standard; **proved in Lean 2026-10-30**,\n  `lean/MOSPFormalization/IndependenceBound.lean`,\n  `pathwidth_le_card_sub_indepNum`, against Mathlib's `indepNum`, standard\n  axioms only; brute-force checked on every graph to 9 vertices the same day]:\n  bags `(V∖I) ∪ {v}`, `v ∈ I`, give `pw ≤ n − α(G)`. Random greedy gives `α ≥ (ln(1+c)/c)·n` whp, so\n  `pw/n ≤ 1 − ln(1+c)/c`. (The greedy constant is a classical result; its\n  reference was not re-checked.) As `c → ∞`, `α ≈ 2n ln c / c` (Frieze 1990),\n  so `1 − pw/n ≥ (2 − o(1)) ln c / c`.\n\nSo for large `c`: **`1 − pw/n ∈ [(2 − o(1)) ln c/c, (4 + o(1)) ln c/c]`**\n[derived], the order of `n − pw` is `Θ(n log c / c)`, and the constant in front\n(between 2 and 4) is open.\n\n---\n\n====7a====\n### (a) Bounds on `pw/n` (and `tw/n`) for `G(n, c/n)`, by `c`\n\nLB is the best lower bound, UB the best upper bound, both whp as `n → ∞`, with\n`o(1)` terms dropped. \"PS\" is Perarnau–Serra's tw bound `1 − √(9 ln 3/(2c))`, a\nlower bound on pw too. \"FM\" is our pw first moment `β*(c)` (§1.4). UB is the\nminimum of cyclomatic, Kneis `c/11.54` and `1 − ln(1+c)/c`. The empirical column\nis Gogate–Dechter's exact **tw**/n (a lower estimate of pw/n) at finite `n`.\n\n| `c` | status | LB on pw/n | source | UB on pw/n | source | G–D exact tw/n |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| < 1 | sublinear | `pw = (1+o(1)) log₃ log n` | derived; `O(log log n)` pub (td) | — | — | — |\n| 1 | critical | `pw → ∞`, `≤ (2/3) log₃ n` | derived | — | — | — |\n| `1+ε`, `ε → 0`, `ε³n → ∞` | `Θ(ε³n)` | `0.02 ε³` | DEK §5 (tw) | `≈ 0.67 ε³` (pw), `1.34 ε³` (tw, DEK) | derived / DEK | — |\n| 1.1 | linear, constant unknown | `r(c) > 0`, implicit | LLO, PS, DEK | 0.0006 | cyclomatic | — |\n| 1.5 | \" | implicit | \" | 0.037 | cyclomatic | — |\n| 2 | \" | implicit | \" | 0.085 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.072 (n=50) |\n| 2.5 | \" | implicit (Gao: linear for c > 2.146) | Gao 2012 | 0.132 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 3 | \" | 0.009 | FM | 0.183 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 4 | \" | 0.061 | FM | 0.285 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.216 (n=50), 0.252 (n=25) |\n| 5 | \" | 0.118 | FM (PS: 0.006) | 0.378 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 6 | \" | 0.171 | FM (PS: 0.092) | 0.458 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.346 (n=50) |\n| 8 | \" | 0.263 | FM (PS: 0.214) | 0.576 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.407 (n=50), 0.472 (n=25) |\n| 10 | \" | 0.337 | FM (PS: 0.297) | 0.656 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 12 | \" | 0.397 | FM (PS: 0.358) | 0.711 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.552 (n=50), 0.604 (n=25) |\n| 16 | \" | 0.488 | FM (PS: 0.444) | 0.782 | E–M Cor 5.2 | 0.674 (n=50), 0.720 (n=25) |\n| 20 | \" | 0.554 | FM (PS: 0.503) | 0.826 | E–M Cor 5.2 | — |\n| 50 | \" | 0.761 | FM (PS: 0.686) | 0.921 | \" | — |\n| 100 | \" | 0.857 | FM (PS: 0.778) | 0.954 | \" | — |\n| `c → ∞` | | `1 − (4+o(1)) ln c/c` | FM | `1 − (2−o(1)) ln c/c` | `α` (Frieze) | — |\n| `p` const. | | `n − (4+o(1)) log_b n` | derived (pub: `n − O(√(n/p))`, PS) | `n − (2−o(1)) log_b n` | `n − α` | — |\n\nNo sharp constant is known at any `c`. Between `c = 1` and `c ≈ 2.8` there is\n**no explicit numerical lower bound at all**, only existence of `r(c) > 0`. The\nupper/lower gap is a factor of about 2–20 throughout the interesting range `2 ≤ c ≤ 10`\n(the UB column is Edwards–McDermid's degree-sequence bound evaluated on Poisson(c)\ndegrees, `reports/random_graph_pathwidth_literature_2.md`, 2026-11-01; it replaced the\nedge-count and cyclomatic values at `2 ≤ c ≤ 20`).\nGogate–Dechter's finite-`n` treewidths sit inside every interval, and they fall\nwith `n` (`c = 8`: 0.472 at `n = 25`, 0.407 at `n = 50`), which shows how large\nthe finite-size effects are at these sizes.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Hcwi8cUParq5vZNWNNqpHJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
