{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RETTNpCHXsFRLhZjHN3TNG","type":"tool_result","content":"\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n\nSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\nare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\nconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in\nMathlib and is proved there from the binomial theorem. Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}\nis proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently. Theorem~\\ref{thm:separator} is a Lean\ntheorem with its inner probability as a finite weighted count; the\nclosed-pair count is a Lean theorem in both its pair-summed and its\nsize-summed form, with the independence step a product over disjoint blocks\nof pairs; the constants\n$\\beta_{\\mathrm{sep}}$ rest on a cited large-deviation rate, and every rate we\nfitted is called fitted.\n\nThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\n====\n106:\\leanentry{emptypair}{Step~1 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: at every position of every layout the prefix and the inactive suffix are an empty pair, so for every $j\\le n$ some $j$-set has closed neighbourhood of size at most $j+\\pw$ (Harper's bound in layout form), and $n-2s^{*}-2\\le\\pw$ for the empty-pair index $s^{*}$} \\leannames{exists_empty_pair_of_layout, exists_closedNbhd_card_le, exists_empty_pair, card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth} \\\\\n107:\\leanentry{firstmoment}{Step~2 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: the number of graphs on $n$ labelled vertices of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$, $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$, and the $G(n,p)$-weighted sum of those graphs is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$; there are $2^{\\binom n2}$ graphs on $n$ labelled vertices} \\leannames{card_pathwidth_le_le, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le, card_emptyPairGraphs, card_simpleGraph} \\\\\n109:\\leanentry{clique-cover}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}: for independent uniform patterns of given sizes, the probability that the instance's MOSP value is at most $k+1$ (its pathwidth at most $k$) is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_j N(s_j)/\\binom n{s_j}$, $N(s)$ the number of $s$-sets missing $A$ or missing $R$} \\leannames{prob_coversOfMospValueLe_le, prob_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_filter_avoid} \\\\\n110:\\leanentry{coupling}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}, the coupling: if every pattern of one cover is contained in the corresponding pattern of another, every empty pair of the larger cover's graph is an empty pair of the smaller's, and the pathwidth is monotone} \\leannames{isEmptyPair_coverGraph_of_subset, pathwidth_coverGraph_mono} \\\\\n113:\\leanentry{mono}{Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}: the MOSP value is monotone under adding requirements (Yanasse's subinstance monotonicity)} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_mono} \\\\\n118:\\leanentry{closedpair}{The closed pair (Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}): $R\\subseteq V\\setminus N[A]$ iff $(A,R)$ is disjoint and empty; one round of the alternating closure $R^{*}=V\\setminus N[A]$, $A^{*}=V\\setminus N[R^{*}]$ is monotone on empty pairs, lands on a closed pair from every $A$, is idempotent, has separator $N(A^{*})\\cap N(R^{*})$ and keeps the sizes; a pair is closed iff it is empty and every outside vertex sees both sides; a layout of width $k<n$ forces a closed empty pair with sides of at least the forced sizes} \\leannames{subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff, subset_closeStep, closeStep_isClosedPair, closeStep_closeStep, sdiff_eq_inter_boundary_of_isClosedPair, card_closeStep, isClosedPair_iff_sees_both, exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le} \\\\\n119:\\leanentry{weightedcount}{The weighted count (Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}): for any weight $1\\le Z(P)\\le\\#\\{\\text{empty pairs}\\}$ on the empty pairs, $\\sum_P \\mathbf 1[P\\ \\text{empty}]/Z(P)\\ge\\mathbf 1[\\text{some pair is empty}]$, so the $G(n,p)$-weighted sum of the graphs of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $E[\\sum_P \\mathbf 1[P\\ \\text{empty}]/Z(G,P)]$ over the exact-size pairs; with $Z$ the number of flips of $P$ (exchanging equally many vertices isolated in $G[A]$ and $G[R]$ gives distinct empty pairs of the same sizes), the flip-weighted count} \\leannames{weightedCount_ge_indicator, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le_weighted, flipPair_isEmptyPair, flipPair_injOn, card_flipPair, card_flips_le, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le_flipWeighted} \\\\\n121:\\leanentry{theoremCl}{The closed-pair count (Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}), the union bound: the weighted sum of the graphs of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $\\sum_{(A,R)}\\Pr[(A,R)\\ \\text{is closed}]$ over the ordered disjoint pairs with $|A|\\ge\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$, $|R|\\ge\\lfloor(n-k)/2\\rfloor$; each term evaluated in the next row} \\leannames{weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le_sum_closedPairs} \\\\\n====\n [6] F. Della Croce, M. J. Kaminski and V. Th. Paschos, An exact algorithm for MAX-\n     CUT in sparse graphs, Oper. Res. Lett. 35 (2007), 403–408.\n [7] K. J. Edwards and G. E. Farr, Fragmentability of graphs, J. Combin. Theory (Ser.\n     B) 82 (2001) 30–37.\n [8] K. J. Edwards and G. E. Farr, Improved upper bounds for planarization and series-\n     parallelization of average degree bounded graphs, Electronic Journal of Combina-\n     torics 19 (2) (2012) #P25.\n [9] K. J. Edwards and G. E. Farr, Graph fragmentability, in: L. W. Beineke and R.\n     J. Wilson (eds.), Topics in Structural Graph Theory, Encyclopedia of Mathematics\n     and its Applications No. 147, Cambridge University Press, 2013, pp. 203–218. ISBN\n     978-0-521-80231-4.\n[10] F. V. Fomin, S. Gaspers, S. Saurabh and A. A. Stepanov, On Two Techniques of\n     Combining Branching and Treewidth, A