{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgdciMP3Gd27zGutYWfYn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019NKLbrX8HKj5CNW85bENxZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'; s=open(p).read()\n\nold1 = r'''Three more things are known. The best published upper bound on the pathwidth\nof $G(n,c/n)$ is the degree-sequence bound of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015},\nwhich on Poisson degrees gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$ at $c=3,6,10$,\nso the measured slopes sit near the middle of the sandwich between\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ and it. The prefix-at-$(1-\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the'''\nnew1 = r'''Three more things are known. The best published upper bound on the pathwidth\nof $G(n,c/n)$ is the degree-sequence bound of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015},\nwhich on Poisson degrees gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$ at $c=3,6,10$,\nso the measured slopes sit near the middle of the sandwich between\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ and it. We did not take that bound on trust. Its statement,\ntheir Corollary~5.2, is $\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_{v}g(d_{r(G)}(v))+\\tau(n)+O(\\log n)$\nfor every graph $G$ on $n$ vertices, where $g(d)=0$ for $d\\le2$, $g(3)=1/6$\nand $d\\,g(d)=(d-2)g(d-1)+g(d-2)+1$; $r(G)$ is the series-parallel reduction\nof $G$, in which vertices of degree at most two are deleted or suppressed\nuntil every degree is $0$ or at least $3$; and $\\tau(n)=o(n)$ is the error\nterm of the cubic base case of \\citet[Theorem~5]{FominHoie2006}. The paper\ngives no constant for either lower-order term. We evaluated the main term on\nevery graph of the record whose pathwidth is proved, 63,330 graphs on 1 to\n2,916 vertices (the pilot, the dataset of Section~\\ref{sec:data} and the\ncertified random covers): it is at least the pathwidth on 61.8\\% of them,\nand the pathwidth exceeds it by at most~5, on the 202-vertex extremal\ntrees, and by at most~4.34 where the reduction is non-empty, on the Coxeter\ngraph. The explicit budget read off Fomin and H{\\o}ie's theorem exceeds\nthese deficits by a factor of 2.2 at ten vertices and 100 at 2,916, so the\ncorollary holds everywhere on record and, at these sizes, says nothing a\ngraph could violate; the constant the record needs is measured, not the\npaper's. On the pilot the degree-histogram term per vertex is within $0.012$\nof the Poisson value at every cell, which is the check behind the three\nnumbers above. We also formalised the theorem. Lemma~5.1 and Corollary~5.2\nare Lean theorems~\\leanref{reduction-theorem}~\\leanref{degree-bound},\nconditional on the two results of the literature the proof rests on, each\nstated as a named proposition that no theorem asserts: the cubic base case,\n$\\pw\\le N/6-1/6+\\tau(N)$ for connected cubic graphs on $N$ vertices with a\nbudget~$\\tau$ \\citep[Theorem~5, whose proof bisects by][and is not\nformalised here]{FominHoie2006,MonienPreis2006}, and the reduction lemma\n$\\pw(H)\\le\\pw(r(H))+\\rho(|V(H)|)$ with a budget~$\\rho$\n\\citep{KneisMolleRichterRossmanith2009}; $\\tau$ is taken monotone and\nnon-negative. Their Theorem~3.2, the reduction theorem, is unconditional:\nits step inequality is proved in all four cases of the paper, and a local\ncommutation lemma replaces the appeal to the uniqueness of the reduced\ngraph. With the budgets explicit the bound reads\n$\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_v g(d_G(v))+\\tau(n)+3\\rho(n)+1$, with $\\tau$ once, $\\rho$ once\nper use of the reduction lemma and one for the deleted vertex of a basic\ncomponent of the second kind; the paper's $\\tau(n)+O(\\log n)$ is this with\n$\\rho=O(\\log n)$. This is a formalisation of a published theorem with its\nlower-order term made explicit; nothing in it is new. Theorem~5.3 of the\nsame paper, the average-degree form, rests on an averaging lemma of\n\\citet{EdwardsFarr2012} that we checked on the record (it holds on all\n52,747 graphs where its hypothesis applies, and fails on 1,431 disconnected\ngraphs of average degree below~5, where it does not) and did not formalise.\nThe prefix-at-$(1-\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the'''\nassert old1 in s; s = s.replace(old1, new1)\n\nold2 = r'''and $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on'''\nnew2 = r'''and $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, verified on every proved graph of\nthe record and formalised conditional on its two cited inputs as described\nabove, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on'''\nassert old2 in s; s = s.replace(old2, new2)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='paper1/latex/sec9a_appendix_lean.tex'; s=open(p).read()\nanchor = s[s.index(r'\\leanentry{theoremClproduct}'):]\nanchor = anchor[:anchor.index('\\\\\\\\\\n')+3]\nrows = r'''\\leanentry{reduction-theorem}{Theorem~3.2 of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, the reduction theorem\n  (Appendix~\\ref{app:random}): every graph with every degree $0$ or at least $3$ has a vertex set $X$\n  such that $G-X$ reduces to a non-empty basic graph $B$ with $|X|+f^-(B)\\le f^-(G)$; unconditional,\n  with the step inequality in all four cases and a local commutation lemma in place of the appeal to\n  the uniqueness of the reduced graph} \\leannames{exists_reduction_set, one_add_potentialMinus_le, SPReduces.isolateSet_comm, potential_isolate} \\\\\n\\leanentry{degree-bound}{Lemma~5.1 and Corollary~5.2 of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015}\n  (Appendix~\\ref{app:random}): $\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_v g(d_G(v))+\\tau(n)+3\\rho(n)+1$ for every $G$,\n  conditional on two cited results stated as named propositions and never asserted --- the cubic\n  base case \\path{CubicPathwidthBound} with budget $\\tau$ \\citep[Theorem~5]{FominHoie2006} and the\n  reduction lemma \\path{ReductionPathwidthBound} with budget $\\rho$\n  \\citep{KneisMolleRichterRossmanith2009} --- and on $\\tau$ monotone and non-negative; the paper\n  gives no constants} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_histogram_of_hypotheses, pathwidth_le_degreeSum_of_hypotheses, pathwidth_le_potentialMinus_of_hypotheses, pathwidth_basic_le, CubicPathwidthBound, ReductionPathwidthBound} \\\\\n'''\ns = s.replace(anchor, anchor + rows, 1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint(\"paper edited\")\nEOF\ngrep -n 'leanentry{reduction-theorem}\\|leanentry{degree-bound}' paper1/latex/sec9a_appendix_lean.tex | cut -c1-60\n(nice -n 19 make -C paper1/latex > /tmp/em_make.log 2>&1; echo \"make exit $?\"; tail -3 /tmp/em_make.log; nice -n 19 make -C paper1/latex check 2>&1 | tail -2)","description":"Apply the paper edits (Appendix E paragraph, closing sentence, two Appendix A rows) and rebuild the paper","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1784,"cache_read_input_tokens":170338,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1784,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97b23e8b-c280-4820-b777-f4948c9a83ab","uuid":"44900c64-c0b8-4a89-851b-8647e0f1553b","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:20:08.390Z","request_id":"req_011CgdciLTUvC2ucYzPq7ykb","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019NKLbrX8HKj5CNW85bENxZ":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'; s=open(p).read()\n\nold1 = r'''Three more things are known. The best published upper bound on the pathwidth\nof $G(n,c/n)$ is the degree-sequence bound of \\citet{EdwardsMcDermid2015},\nwhich on Poisson degrees gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$ at $c=3,6,10$,\nso the measured slopes sit near the middle of the sandwich be