{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgdcq2SDWP6E3RhaYkXLm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UGXGXDUvsUXXSbXhUSnQBR","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'; s=open(p).read()\ni = s.index('We did not take that bound on trust.')\nj = s.index('The prefix-at-$(1-\\\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the', i)\nnew = r'''We did not take that bound on trust. Their Corollary~5.2 states\n$\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_{v}g(d_{r(G)}(v))+\\tau(n)+O(\\log n)$ for every graph on $n$\nvertices, with $g$ a weight function ($g(d)=0$ for $d\\le2$, $g(3)=1/6$,\n$d\\,g(d)=(d-2)g(d-1)+g(d-2)+1$), $r(G)$ the series-parallel reduction of\n$G$, and $\\tau(n)=o(n)$ the error term of the cubic base case\n\\citep[Theorem~5]{FominHoie2006}; the paper gives no constant for either\nlower-order term. On every graph of the record with a proved pathwidth,\n63,330 graphs on 1 to 2,916 vertices (the pilot, the dataset of\nSection~\\ref{sec:data} and the certified random covers), the main term alone\nis at least the pathwidth on 61.8\\%, and the pathwidth exceeds it by at\nmost~5 (the 202-vertex extremal trees), by at most~4.34 where the reduction\nis non-empty (the Coxeter graph). Fomin and H{\\o}ie's explicit budget\nexceeds these deficits by a factor of 2.2 at ten vertices and 100 at 2,916:\nthe corollary holds everywhere on record and, at these sizes, says nothing a\ngraph could violate. On the pilot the histogram term per vertex is within\n$0.012$ of its Poisson value in every cell. Lemma~5.1 and Corollary~5.2 are\nalso Lean theorems~\\leanref{reduction-theorem}~\\leanref{degree-bound},\nconditional on the two cited results the proof rests on, each a named\nproposition that no theorem asserts: the cubic base case\n$\\pw\\le N/6-1/6+\\tau(N)$ for connected cubic graphs with a budget~$\\tau$\n\\citep[Theorem~5]{FominHoie2006}, whose bisection step is\n\\citet{MonienPreis2006}, and the reduction lemma\n$\\pw(H)\\le\\pw(r(H))+\\rho(|V(H)|)$ with a budget~$\\rho$\n\\citep{KneisMolleRichterRossmanith2009}; $\\tau$ is taken monotone and\nnon-negative. Their Theorem~3.2, the reduction theorem, is unconditional,\nwith the step inequality proved in all four cases and a local commutation\nlemma in place of the appeal to the uniqueness of the reduced graph. With\nthe budgets explicit the bound reads\n$\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_v g(d_G(v))+\\tau(n)+3\\rho(n)+1$, $\\rho$ once per use of the\nreduction lemma and $1$ for the deleted vertex of a basic component of the\nsecond kind; the paper's $\\tau(n)+O(\\log n)$ is this with $\\rho=O(\\log n)$.\nThis is a formalisation of a published theorem with its lower-order term\nmade explicit, not a new result. Theorem~5.3, the average-degree form,\nrests on an averaging lemma of \\citet{EdwardsFarr2012} that holds on the\n52,747 graphs of the record where its hypothesis applies and fails on 1,431\ndisconnected ones where it does not; it is not formalised.\n'''\ns = s[:i] + new + s[j:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\n(nice -n 19 make -C paper1/latex > /tmp/em_make.log 2>&1; echo \"make exit $?\"; nice -n 19 make -C paper1/latex check 2>&1 | grep Pages)\npdftotext -layout paper1/latex/main.pdf /tmp/em_main.txt; python3 - <<'EOF'\nt=open('/tmp/em_main.txt').read()\ni=t.index('We did not take that bound'); j=t.index('The prefix-at-', i)\nseg=t[i:j]; print('paragraph: lines', seg.count('\\n'), 'words', len(seg.split()), '| total words', len(t.split()))\npages=t.split('\\f')\nfor k,pg in enumerate(pages):\n    if 'We did not take that bound' in pg: print('page', k+1, 'text lines', len([l for l in pg.split('\\n') if l.strip()]))\nh=open('/tmp/em_head.txt').read().split('\\f')\ndef last_page(pages,key):\n    r=None\n    for k,pg in enumerate(pages):\n        if key in pg: r=k+1\n    return r\nfor key in ['Formal names','The pathwidth of sparse random graphs','What is checked, and what is known','References']:\n    print(key, 'HEAD', last_page(h,key), 'now', last_page(pages,key))\nEOF","description":"Rebuild the paper with the shortened paragraph and measure its length and the page shifts","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1121,"cache_read_input_tokens":179621,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1121,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97b23e8b-c280-4820-b777-f4948c9a83ab","uuid":"9237c29d-b5bb-43d4-ab16-e64c54682d4d","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:22:26.387Z","request_id":"req_011Cgdcq258ZAoJEEUmfp4s4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UGXGXDUvsUXXSbXhUSnQBR":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'; s=open(p).read()\ni = s.index('We did not take that bound on trust.')\nj = s.index('The prefix-at-$(1-\\\\varepsilon)n$ variant of the', i)\nnew = r'''We did not take that bound on trust. Their Corollary~5.2 states\n$\\pw(G)\\le\\sum_{v}g(d_{r(G)}(v))+\\tau(n)+O(\\log n)$ for every graph on $n$\nvertices, with $g$ a weight function ($g(d)=0$ for $d\\le2$, $g(3)=1/6$,\n$d\\,g(d)=(d-2)g(d-1)+g(d-2)+1$), $r(G)$ the series-parallel reduction of\n$G$, and $\\tau(n)=o(n)$ the error term of the cubic base case\n\\citep[Theorem~5]{FominHoie2006}; the paper gives no constant for either\nlower-order term. On every graph of the record with a proved pathwidth,\n63,330 graphs on 1 to 2,916 vertices (the pilot, the dataset of\nSection~\\ref{sec:data} and the certified random covers), the main term alone\nis at least the pathwidth on 61.8\\%, and the pathwidth exceeds it by at\nmost~5 (the 202-vertex extremal trees), by at most~4.34 where the reduction\nis non-empty (the Coxeter graph). Fomin and H{\\o}ie's explicit budge